<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Achirimbi II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1932">1932</a>–<a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Achirimbi II was Fon of Bafut, a kingdom in the Grassfields of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, from 1932 to 1968. He ruled under British administration and into independent Cameroon. He became widely known abroad through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerald-durrell">Gerald Durrell</a>'s book <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-bafut-beagles">The Bafut Beagles</a> (1954), which described Durrell's animal-collecting stay at his court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/achirimbi-ii.jpg" alt="Achirimbi II" width="320" height="303" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fotograaf / photographer: Drs. G.W. (Gerrit Willem) Grootenhuis (1930) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_De_Fon_van_Bafut_poseert_bij_de_mierenval_op_zijn_erf_TMnr_20012968.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Achirimbi II was the tenth Fon of Bafut, ruling the town and its surrounding lands, the Fondom of Bafut, in a semi-autonomous fashion. He succeeded Abumbi I, and his reign spanned the handover of the British trust territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-cameroons">Southern Cameroons</a>, part of British Cameroons, to independent Cameroon.</p><p>His sympathy for the British cause set him apart from other Fons. In 1946 the British government awarded him a Certificate of Honour as a record of his valuable services to his own country and people and to the British Government, and of his loyal help to the administration in the maintenance of good order. He used this favourable standing to claim, as compensation for his loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, the residual powers over land and disputes that the sub-chiefs, the Atangchuo, had kept. When British Cameroons chose in 1961 between joining independent Cameroon and independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, he remarked that it was a choice between &quot;the Fire and the Deep Sea&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The naturalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerald-durrell">Gerald Durrell</a> visited Bafut on his animal-collecting expeditions in 1949 and 1957, and the Fon gave him substantial help. Durrell's books on these journeys, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-bafut-beagles">The Bafut Beagles</a> and A Zoo in My Luggage, portray the Fon mostly with humour, lampooning his polygamy, his anglophile outlook and his high tolerance for alcohol, but also in more flattering ways. During the second expedition the Fon presented Durrell with ceremonial dress and a staff.</p><p>Many considered Achirimbi II progressive and willing to experiment with new ideas, and both colonial administrators and nationalist politicians treated him with respect. He was succeeded as Fon by Abumbi II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Achirimbi II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Fon still reigns; <b>Achirimbi II</b> became widely known through Gerald Durrell's book The Bafut Beagles.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bafut">Kingdom of Bafut</a></li></ul></section>
